# Contradiction
#
# Catharsis never collapses opposing feelings into a net position.  There is no
# "love minus anger".  Both charges sit on the same edge, and their overlap is a
# quantity in its own right: `tension`.
#
# Tension is not an error state, and it is not inert either.  Action pressure is
# divided by `1 + tension`, so an agent holding a genuine contradiction is
# *slower* than one that is merely indifferent -- ambivalence looks like
# paralysis from the outside, and that falls straight out of the arithmetic
# rather than out of a rule about ambivalence.
#
# Alice below is not confused.  She is in a well-defined state that a language
# with booleans cannot represent.

alice = agent
bob = agent

love alice bob 0.8
anger alice bob 0.6

trust alice bob 0.4
doubt alice bob 0.7

hope alice bob 0.9
fear alice bob 0.3

observe alice

# Let it run. The contradictions do not resolve themselves -- the coupling
# matrix moves them around, but nothing in the runtime is trying to tidy them
# away, and Alice stays slow while she holds them.
tick 10
observe alice

# `acceptance` is the only thing that collapses a contradiction, and it is not
# free: whichever side loses is paid out as grief.
acceptance alice
tick 3
observe alice
